<Layout title="Stones Between" description="200 roads between two opposite words, three stones on each road from four machines; cell width is how much they disagree">
  <div class="lt-page">
    <h1>Stones Between</h1>
    <p>200 roads between two opposite words. four machines placed three stones on each road, blind to each other. (Fable went first, in this chat.)</p>
    <p>width = disagreement. where the machines argue, the cell spreads out. where they agree, it shrinks to almost nothing. every row is measured on its own.</p>
    <p id="legend"><font color="#000000">Fable 5.1</font> · <font color="#0000ff">Opus 5.5</font> · <font color="#ff69b4">Sonnet 5.5</font> · <font color="#ff8c00">Haiku 5.5</font> · <font color="#008000"><b>green = shared word</b></font></p>
    <p><button type="button" id="colorBtn">color on (click for off)</button></p>
    <p>all four agree: 24 / 600. at least two share a word: 399 / 600.</p>
    <p>teachers call this a <a href="https://www.readingrockets.org/sites/default/files/migrated/content/pdfs/26_semantic-gradients.pdf">semantic gradient</a>: frigid, cool, tepid, hot, boiling. language researchers ask whether models can <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.02686">put words like warm, hot, scalding in order</a>, and find they <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.16426">lose the thread across a whole scale</a>. here nobody is graded. the machines just walk, and the table shows where their walks split.</p>
    <p>this is not the latent space. the latent space is the numbers inside each model, and those stay hidden. walking it for real means taking the vectors for two words and stepping along the line between them, and the in-between points often <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.11381">decode to nothing</a>. <a href="https://thestrangeloop.com/2017/experimental-creative-writing-with-the-vectorized-word.html">Allison Parrish</a> writes poems out of midpoints like these, between how words sound and how they are spelled. what's on this table is a shadow of that space: the words each machine says out loud when asked to walk it.</p>
    <p>researchers have found that <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19361">language models answer more like each other than people do</a>. at the level of a single word, these four mostly don't. and they are four models from one lab, so machines from other families would likely argue more.</p>
    <p>i really like the aesthetic of plain html tables: 1px black rules, Times New Roman, the browser's own grey button, blue links. no design on top, just the table doing the work. so the piece is built that way.</p>
    <div class="lt-wrap"><div id="tb" style="min-width:640px"></div></div>

    <h2>born → die, recursive</h2>
    <p>one road, walked again and again. each machine placed three stones between born and die, then three more between every pair of its own stones, then once more. 65 points each, still blind to each other.</p>
    <p>so the table recurses too. every gap between two stones is its own table, set inside the one above it, indented one step. the first three stones are the biggest words; the last ones, three tables deep, the smallest. each line holds what all four machines put at that spot.</p>
    <p>read across, position by position: all four agree 0 / 63. at least two share a word: 4 / 63. anywhere on the road, only "newborn" turns up in three of the four.</p>
    <p>the middle of the road: live, prime, work, prime.</p>
    <p>two dialects show up. Fable and Sonnet walk in verbs: crawl, walk, learn, wither. Opus and Haiku walk in stages: crawling, walking, puberty, career. the same life, told in different grammar, so a lot of the disagreement is only in the grammar.</p>
    <div class="lt-wrap"><div id="rr"></div></div>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="/scripts/stones-between.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src="/scripts/stones-between-recursive.js"></script>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* kept as near-plain HTML: white page, Times, 1px black rules */
  .lt-page {
    background: #fff;
    color: #000;
    font-family: "Times New Roman", Times, serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    padding: 8px max(16px, env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px)) 32px;
  }

  .lt-page h1 {
    font-family: "Times New Roman", Times, serif;
    font-size: 2em;
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    margin: 0.67em 0;
    /* no ink bleed / bloom on this title: it stays plain HTML */
    filter: none;
  }

  .lt-page h2 {
    font-family: "Times New Roman", Times, serif;
    font-size: 1.5em;
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: normal;
    margin: 2em 0 0.83em;
    filter: none;
  }

  .lt-page p {
    margin: 1em 0;
  }

  /* the recursive road: tables inside tables, each gap one step in */
  .lt-page :global(.rn) {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
  }

  .lt-page :global(.rn td) {
    border: 1px solid #000;
    padding: 4px;
  }

  /* the inner table draws the top, right and bottom edges, so they stay 1px */
  .lt-page :global(.rn td.rg) {
    padding: 0 0 0 2em;
    border-top: 0;
    border-right: 0;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* and sits 1px over its neighbours' rules above and below */
  .lt-page :global(.rn .rn) {
    margin: -1px 0;
  }

  .lt-page a {
    color: #0000ee;
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .lt-page a:visited {
    color: #551a8b;
  }

  .lt-page button {
    font: 13px -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif;
    color: #000;
    background: #efefef;
    border: 1px solid #767676;
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 1px 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .lt-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lt-page :global(.r) {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    table-layout: fixed;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: -1px;
    word-wrap: break-word;
  }

  .lt-page :global(.r td) {
    border: 1px solid #000;
    padding: 4px;
  }

  .lt-page :global(.r:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
